    "body": "Kodiak AI and Charger USA have launched an autonomous trucking service between Dallas and Laredo, Texas , hauling refrigerated goods and dry freight on a 435-mile route. The companies announced the partnership on Thursday, with trucks already carrying loads for consumer goods and food and beverage customers.\nWhat’s Related\nThe service runs between Charger’s terminals in the two cities. Kodiak and Charger completed their first delivery on September 8, making this Kodiak’s first route serving Laredo.\nThe trucks currently operate with a safety driver behind the wheel. The companies plan to remove the driver once Kodiak completes its safety case, a review of the evidence showing that its driving system can operate safely on the route.\nCharger serves customers moving freight within the U.S. and across the Mexican border. The company offers refrigerated transport , truckload shipping , dedicated trucking, warehousing , and cross-border services.\nRELATED: Aurora Triples Driverless Truck Routes Across Texas\nSafety drivers remain in truck\nTrucks on the Dallas-to-Laredo route use the Kodiak Driver, the company’s autonomous driving system. Charger and Kodiak expect to add more routes over time as Charger brings the technology into its own fleet.\n“Charger came to us knowing what they wanted from autonomy and what they need to see before scaling it,” said Don Burnette, founder and CEO of Kodiak.\nKodiak provides its system as a service for customer-owned and operated trucks. That approach allows carriers such as Charger to bring autonomous driving into their existing trucking operations.\nA new route to the border\nLaredo handled more than $350 billion in international trade in 2025, accounting for nearly 40% of U.S. trade with Mexico, according to the announcement. Food, beverages, and other consumer goods moving through the area help replenish retail inventory.\nThe companies did not announce a date for removing safety drivers from the Dallas-to-Laredo route. Kodiak separately plans to launch long-haul driverless service by the end of 2026, with Dallas-to-Houston identified as its initial route.\nKodiak already has driverless trucks operating in the Permian Basin, where customer-owned vehicles have run without a driver since December 2024. The Dallas-to-Laredo partnership adds another Texas freight route as the company works toward driverless highway service.",
